Subject: [PATCH 539/539] DeepSeek V4.1 tokenizer: the DeepSeek API's effort
 aliases

The DeepSeek API accepts reasoning_effort minimal, medium and ultra as aliases
of low (50), high (75) and max (100). The chat protocol already accepts
minimal and medium, but the V4.1 tokenizer knew only low, high, xhigh and max,
so an OpenAI-style client sending reasoning_effort "medium" passed validation
and then got a 400 from the template; "ultra" was refused by the protocol.

- The V4.1 effort table gains the three aliases at their API budgets.
- The chat protocol accepts "ultra" (like "max", DeepSeek-specific). Other
  renderers look named efforts up with a default, so an unknown name stays
  theirs to handle.

Requests with any previously accepted effort render the same prompt.

Tests: the aliases render 50/75/100 through the tokenizer, the encoder's
effort prefix and a chat request; "ultra" and "minimal" turn thinking on; the
invalid-effort cases keep only spellings the API does not accept.

Base: next-candidate e2b2b0b59 (spark-ds41f candidate 9).

Quality: frontend only; prompts of requests without these aliases are
token-identical.

Upstream status: local; not proposed.
